Modifications by Alpha-Leader, 2026-09-01
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

New features:

  * Per-application profiles: a placement (and target monitor) can be saved
    per executable and applied automatically whenever a matching window
    appears. Auto-apply fires once per window, never fights a manual restore,
    and asserts the placement a second time shortly after applying, for games
    that re-take their own size after launch.

  * Global hotkeys (default Ctrl+Alt+B / Ctrl+Alt+R): toggle borderless on the
    focused window, and restore every stripped window, without switching to
    the app. Bindings are configurable.

  * System tray: the close button hides the window to the tray by default
    (configurable), with Show/Hide, Restore all, and Quit in the tray menu,
    and an option to start minimized.

  * Original window frames persist to disk, so Restore Borders works across a
    restart of this app, including after a crash: a saved frame is re-attached
    to a live borderless window matching the same executable and window class.
    Entries expire after seven days.

  * Monitors are remembered by device name rather than enumeration index, so
    saved settings and profiles survive monitors being added or removed.

Architectural changes:

  * Window scanning and auto-apply moved from the UI loop to a dedicated
    watcher thread. eframe stops producing frames while its window is hidden,
    so nothing that must keep working from the tray runs in the frame loop;
    window state is shared behind a mutex, and tray/hotkey handlers act
    directly from the Win32 message pump.

  * The main window is vertically resizable and its content scrolls.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Modifications by Alpha-Leader, 2026-09-01 (continued)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

New features:

  * Cursor lock: a per-profile option confines the mouse to a managed
    window's rectangle while it has focus, re-asserted continuously since
    Windows silently drops the clip on focus changes and similar events.
    Ctrl+Alt+L toggles a session-only lock override for whichever window is
    currently focused, independent of any profile.

  * A focus and topmost watchdog re-asserts a managed window's placement and
    always-on-top state whenever they decay, debounced so it never fights a
    deliberate move or resize by the user.

  * Taskbar auto-hide while gaming: an option to auto-hide the taskbar for as
    long as any window is managed by this app, restoring the prior state
    exactly when the last one closes or the app exits. A window this app
    already owns from a previous run (adopted from a persisted frame, or
    simply still borderless from before) is picked up into management the
    same way a freshly-applied one is, and a taskbar left auto-hidden by a
    hard crash is restored on the next launch if nothing is left to manage.

  * Companion dimming: windows marked as companions dim to a configurable
    brightness while a managed window has focus, and return to full
    brightness while the mouse hovers them, without touching the game's
    focus. A window that manages its own transparency is left alone.

Architectural changes:

  * The watcher thread gained a 250ms heartbeat, active only while a session
    effect (a managed window, an override, a clip, a dim, or a taskbar
    change) exists, alongside the pre-existing 5-second scan.

